# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the register entries to propose (id, title, what, why, tier)
GAPS = [
    ('G01', 'Which crop each field cell held', 'No source read assigns a crop to the fields of the open ground except the Wheatfield and the few fields in claims that can be placed (role exact or nearest). Every other cell takes its crop from the district mix by a seeded draw.', 'The 1860 census schedules and the 1868 damage-claim files are not read; the NPS inventories give field numbers whose outlines are in GIS layers the repository does not hold.', 'plausible-procedural'),
    ('G02', 'Field boundaries inside the open ground', 'The cut lines that divide a block of open ground into field cells are straight cuts across the long axis of each piece, placed by a seed, not a map. Roads, lanes, the railway and the 30 confirmed fence lines bound the blocks; wood, orchard and marsh edges are the Warren outlines.', 'Fence lines other than the 30 confirmed ones are not mapped for 1863 (362 of 392 candidates are modern reconstructions or unchecked).', 'plausible-procedural'),
    ('G03', 'Ripeness and cut date of each grain field', 'Wheat stood nearly ripe on 2 July (documented) and was being cut in the last two weeks of July (documented); the date each field was cut, and rye, barley and oats, are ranged.', 'No farmer\'s diary, harvest notice or photograph of 5-14 July with a dated field was found. The 1863 Compiler and Adams Sentinel (June to August) were searched by keyword, not read page by page; that reading is owed.', 'plausible-procedural'),
    ('G04', 'Grass height and mowing dates of the hay meadows', 'Meadows were grassy on the morning of 2 July; new hay existed by 10 July in Maryland; the mowing date of each meadow is a seeded draw.', 'No source gives a grass height.', 'plausible-procedural'),
    ('G05', 'Fruit state of the orchards', 'Cherries were ripe on 2 and 5 July (documented); apples, peaches, pears and plums have no sourced state; the viewer is told so.', 'Same as EG-0020: no sourced fruit-state account.', 'plausible-procedural'),
    ('G06', 'Species and age of woodlot and orchard trees', 'The family (oak-hickory) is sourced for two woodlots and for Culp\'s Hill, where Bachelder names fine old oak and hickory (1889) and the park\'s report of Big Round Top says mature deciduous and partly coniferous forest; chestnut and black locust are shown present by the fence timber; every share, every height and the age of orchards other than the named young orchards are ranges.', 'The NPS Historic Vegetation Study and the 1860 census are not in the library (register EG-0139, EG-0143); the plan lists "no sourced species list for Culp\'s Hill or Big Round Top": Bachelder\'s two words for Culp\'s Hill are the only species statement found for either.', 'plausible-procedural'),
    ('G07', 'Understory of the ridge forests', 'Farm woodlots had an open understory (documented). Kershaw (2 July) gives thick undergrowth on the stony hill behind the Rose house and a scrubby interval of about 100 yards beyond it (documented, placed by a rule); Greene and Jones call Culp\'s Hill rocky, wooded and heavily timbered, and Bachelder says it was generally covered with a heavy open growth of hardy timber (read as widely spaced trees: no undergrowth value is set from it); a wood of Seminary Ridge is called an open grove (placed on the nearest wood, so inferred). The understory of the rest of Culp\'s Hill, Big Round Top and Houck\'s Ridge is not described; a rocky ravine of trees and undergrowth (3 July) is named but not located.', 'The Official Records reports were searched for timber, forest, undergrowth, scrub and thicket; a report that search did not find was not read through.', 'plausible-procedural'),
    ('G08', 'Individual tree positions', 'Every tree is placed by a seeded scatter inside its polygon, in rows for orchards; only the witness trees named in claims (the large oak at the Trostle lane, the tree at the Codori lane) have a documented place and none of them is placed to better than the polygon.', 'No tree map of 1863 exists.', 'plausible-procedural'),
    ('G09', 'Little Round Top tree cover', 'Carried as three selectable readings; the NPS Cultural Landscape Report supports the first, and a second National Park Service report (about 1940) agrees on the west slope: cut clear a year before the battle (no source given for the date), nearly bare in the Brady photographs taken after it, with the pine and oak the Warren map shows there five years later read as later growth.', 'Two web articles conflict; the reports settle the west slope and the east slope, not every tree. The Brady photographs themselves were not read (G13).', 'inferred'),
    ('G10', 'Vegetation on 19 November 1863', 'Leaf-off is documented by one remark; the field stages are drawn per field from the calendar.', 'The plan lists "No Nov 19 vegetation source".', 'plausible-procedural'),
    ('G11', 'Extent and degree of trampling and tree damage', 'That fields were trampled (2 July, Haskell; the Wheatfield in the OR reports) and that trees were stripped or splintered (Culp\'s Hill, Herbst Woods, the orchards) is documented; the share of each field or stand affected is ranged and follows the troops the theatre draws.', 'No survey of the damage by field exists in the sources read; the 1868 damage claims give acreages destroyed per farm, not where.', 'plausible-procedural'),
    ('G12', 'Damage-claim acreages of farms not yet read', 'Eight farms\' crop acreages are read (McPherson, Wills, Harman, Forney, Slyder, Josiah Benner, Abraham Spangler, Trostle; the Bushman claim names crops without acreage); the Rose, Sherfy, Codori, Weikert, Wentz, Bliss, Rogers, McClean and other claims are not.', 'The claim files are not in the library; the NPS reports quote some of them. Found in the saved reports and not used: the Biesecker claim (12 acres of wheat, 3.5 of corn, 10 of oats) and the Bollinger claim (a tenant\'s share of the crop) in the Eisenhower National Historic Site study, and the McClean claim, whose table has ditto marks; their print is garbled in the saved text, so no acreage is taken from them until the pages are read from the page images.', 'open gap'),
    ('G13', 'Photographs of 5-14 July for stubble and trample state', 'No photograph is used for any vegetation state here. A National Park Service report says the Brady photographs of the post-battle period show the west slope of Little Round Top nearly devoid of trees, and cites photographs for the open character of Devil\'s Den; the photographs themselves were not read.', 'The photo-match catalogue exists (109 records); a field-by-field reading of stubble and trample is owed.', 'open gap'),
    ('G14', 'Farmyards, gardens, potato patches, flax, buckwheat, hedgerows', 'The plan\'s schedule of vegetation states is "per field, woodlot, orchard and yard" (PDF L.7); the layer has fields, woodlots, orchards, marsh, boulder ground and cleared slope, and no yard class: the ground round the farm buildings, the kitchen gardens and the dooryard trees are not drawn. Gardens, potato patches, flax and buckwheat are named in claims as present on the farms, and hedgerows (grown mostly of themselves along fence lines and on rocky ground, of mixed trees, shrubs and briars, in the report\'s general account); none has a place, so none is drawn.', 'No source places them, and no source describes the ground cover of a farmyard in 1863; a yard class would have to be a seeded reconstruction round the farmsteads of the structures layer (plausible-procedural), and is not made here.', 'open gap'),
    ('G15', 'Modern trees in the ground photograph', 'The terrain is draped with a 2022 aerial photograph that shows modern woods and hedgerows; the field decals cover them where a field cell is drawn, but a modern tree that stands on a road verge, a fence line or in the town stays in the picture.', 'The prototype terrain texture is fixed.', 'open gap'),
    ('G16', 'Chestnut and the later changes to the woods', 'American chestnut is drawn as a living tree of the woodlots in ranged shares (a fifth or less of the trees: the NPS says the fences of the period were mostly chestnut and black locust, and that the hickory, chestnut and oak stands of Little Round Top had been harvested several times); the blight that later removed it, which the plan dates to about 1904, is not in any text read, so no blight, no dead chestnut and no modern species list is used, and nothing is drawn of the changes after 1863 that the park reports (replanting, regrowth, clearing: ground claims).', 'The date of the blight is general knowledge, not a retrieved non-lead source (PDF B.10.3 says "verify"); the modern lists the park publishes are of the woods as they are now and are not read into 1863.', 'plausible-procedural'),
    ('G17', 'Sway of the foliage and the crops in the wind', 'The crowns of the trees lean and flutter and the standing crop near the camera runs in waves in the wind of the weather timeline (its speed from the recorded or chosen range, 2 to 6 mph by default, from S to SSW; the weather timeline marks that direction inferred); single leaves, stalks and branches are not drawn, so nothing sways at the scale of a plant.', 'No source gives how far a crown or a stalk moved in the wind of those days: the amount of lean and flutter is a look parameter, not a reading (PDF B.10.3: wind_response is inferred).', 'plausible-procedural'),
]
